WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.497 --> 00:00:03.580
SPEAKER 1: Hey, everybody.

00:00:03.580 --> 00:00:08.010
We are back with the marketing
manager of EatandRun.

00:00:08.010 --> 00:00:09.540
So we got to go over this again.

00:00:09.540 --> 00:00:13.323
EatandRun-- it's an app
that does, what now?

00:00:13.323 --> 00:00:15.240
SPEAKER 2: Well, what
our developers have done

00:00:15.240 --> 00:00:18.780
is design a data-driven dynamic
dining discovery and delivery

00:00:18.780 --> 00:00:21.870
service specializing in
serving, specifically,

00:00:21.870 --> 00:00:26.650
customers and clients
currently doing cardio.

00:00:26.650 --> 00:00:29.920
Our primary purpose is to pair
people with the perfect place

00:00:29.920 --> 00:00:32.470
to provide them a quick
bite, day or night,

00:00:32.470 --> 00:00:37.270
before they're done with
their run, all from the Cloud.

00:00:37.270 --> 00:00:38.080
SPEAKER 1: Ah, OK.

00:00:38.080 --> 00:00:38.800
I see.

00:00:38.800 --> 00:00:44.590
So your app delivers
food to runners.

00:00:44.590 --> 00:00:48.100
SPEAKER 2: Yeah, exactly,
but from the Cloud.

00:00:54.422 --> 00:00:56.740
MAX SALTONSTALL: OK,
so that interview

00:00:56.740 --> 00:00:58.630
has 1.8 million views.

00:00:58.630 --> 00:01:00.770
Now traffic is going
through the roof.

00:01:00.770 --> 00:01:03.260
I'm prouder than ever to
have started this together.

00:01:03.260 --> 00:01:06.250
But all this attention is also
going to mean some challenges

00:01:06.250 --> 00:01:07.570
for our technology.

00:01:07.570 --> 00:01:08.553
Right?

00:01:08.553 --> 00:01:09.970
BUKOLA AYODELE:
Yeah, that's good.

00:01:09.970 --> 00:01:11.560
And now more people
around the world

00:01:11.560 --> 00:01:14.800
can grab food while jogging,
walking, or marathon training

00:01:14.800 --> 00:01:16.462
in these uncertain times.

00:01:16.462 --> 00:01:18.670
We should definitely make
sure our infrastructure can

00:01:18.670 --> 00:01:21.330
handle the surge of traffic.

00:01:21.330 --> 00:01:22.950
MAX SALTONSTALL: Sure, sure.

00:01:22.950 --> 00:01:24.310
Everything will be fine.

00:01:24.310 --> 00:01:27.060
We always planned for scale.

00:01:27.060 --> 00:01:29.640
Walk, then run,
then eat and run.

00:01:29.640 --> 00:01:30.490
Right?

00:01:30.490 --> 00:01:32.940
BUKOLA AYODELE: Mm,
yes, of course, we did.

00:01:32.940 --> 00:01:35.970
At totally big scale.

00:01:35.970 --> 00:01:37.410
MAX SALTONSTALL: Oh, I see.

00:01:37.410 --> 00:01:41.003
So maybe we're not as
ready as I thought.

00:01:41.003 --> 00:01:42.420
Well, for the good
of the company,

00:01:42.420 --> 00:01:44.045
for the good of the
people of the world

00:01:44.045 --> 00:01:46.930
and their ability to
satisfy cravings on the go,

00:01:46.930 --> 00:01:49.593
we have to figure out
a way to scale up.

00:01:49.593 --> 00:01:50.760
BUKOLA AYODELE: Don't worry.

00:01:50.760 --> 00:01:52.830
We're running on the
most scalable cloud

00:01:52.830 --> 00:01:54.130
architecture in the world.

00:01:54.130 --> 00:01:55.770
And I'm sure that
we have the capacity

00:01:55.770 --> 00:01:58.170
to handle any traffic spikes.

00:01:58.170 --> 00:02:00.480
They deal with enormous
denial of service attacks

00:02:00.480 --> 00:02:02.800
before breakfast.

00:02:02.800 --> 00:02:04.180
MAX SALTONSTALL: Breakfast, yes.

00:02:04.180 --> 00:02:05.640
OK, but let's do some research.

00:02:08.789 --> 00:02:13.500
This article on the internet
has suggested some changes

00:02:13.500 --> 00:02:15.100
that I think we can make.

00:02:15.100 --> 00:02:17.610
MAX SALTONSTALL: OK, one
option is load balancers

00:02:17.610 --> 00:02:19.350
and managed instance groups.

00:02:19.350 --> 00:02:21.540
With all this traffic,
we could deploy

00:02:21.540 --> 00:02:24.120
load balancers to distribute
load more efficiently

00:02:24.120 --> 00:02:25.980
across our front end servers.

00:02:25.980 --> 00:02:29.340
Then on top of that, we set
up managed instance groups

00:02:29.340 --> 00:02:30.520
for our virtual machine.

00:02:30.520 --> 00:02:35.490
So each group can scale up
or resize based on demand.

00:02:35.490 --> 00:02:37.920
MAX SALTONSTALL: And option
two looks like Cloud CDN.

00:02:37.920 --> 00:02:41.040
We could speed up the delivery
of our assets to our clients

00:02:41.040 --> 00:02:44.580
with a content delivery
network and added caching.

00:02:44.580 --> 00:02:47.760
Looks like using Cloud CDN helps
us put the static assets closer

00:02:47.760 --> 00:02:50.280
to those who need them, reducing
the need for those requests

00:02:50.280 --> 00:02:52.020
to hit our compute back ends.

00:02:52.020 --> 00:02:54.570
Then we could add caching
for static content stored

00:02:54.570 --> 00:02:55.470
on the CDN.

00:02:55.470 --> 00:02:57.750
So our sign-up page for
new eaters and runners

00:02:57.750 --> 00:02:59.638
would probably load faster.

00:02:59.638 --> 00:03:00.930
BUKOLA AYODELE: It's up to you.

00:03:00.930 --> 00:03:03.210
What should we prioritize
for the engineering team

00:03:03.210 --> 00:03:06.780
first since we only have limited
time and resources to handle

00:03:06.780 --> 00:03:09.150
this big traffic spike?

00:03:09.150 --> 00:03:12.420
Option one-- load balancers
and managed instance groups

00:03:12.420 --> 00:03:14.520
to scale up our back serving.

00:03:14.520 --> 00:03:16.980
Option two-- content
delivery network

00:03:16.980 --> 00:03:19.500
and caching to speed
up our asset delivery.

00:03:19.500 --> 00:03:21.840
MAX SALTONSTALL:
So what will it be?

00:03:21.840 --> 00:03:25.190
[MUSIC PLAYING]

